WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.160
(crowd chanting)

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.180
- [Narrator] When the Supreme Court

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.980
overturned Roe versus Wade,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.700
it did more than just allow
states to ban abortion.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.980
It showed how this court views rights

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.800
not explicitly listed in the Constitution.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.350
- It's really difficult to
exaggerate the importance

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.481
of this decision.

00:00:17.481 --> 00:00:20.280
The court has pulled a complete 180,

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.340
going from recognizing
a constitutional right,

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.070
an individual right to
saying 50 years later,

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.410
no, we were mistaken.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:29.550
- [Narrator] Here's the legal reasoning

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.850
behind that 180 and why even
though the court's opinion

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.580
made clear it applies solely to abortion,

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.160
some legal experts think
the same methodology

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.674
could be used to revise other rulings.

00:00:40.674 --> 00:00:43.230
(pensive music)

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.910
The Framers wrote the Bill of Rights,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.740
the amendments to the constitution

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.410
to well, list the rights Americans have.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.610
The right to free speech,

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:53.250
to bear arms, to not have
soldiers in your house

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.140
during peacetime, you know them.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.110
And at some point,
specifically at the ninth one,

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.090
they wrapped it up by saying this list

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.400
isn't exhaustive and
there are other rights

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.940
not named here that cannot
be denied or disparaged.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.100
Or in 18th century terminology,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.530
the rights not in the enumeration.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.150
- So the Ninth Amendment
is there essentially

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.460
to say that there are unenumerated rights.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.900
That the list in the Bill
of Rights at the time

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.120
was not intended to be exclusive.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.610
And so then the difficult
part becomes figuring out

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.800
what those unenumerated rights are.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:27.630
- [Narrator] The Supreme
Court has long seen the right

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:28.830
to privacy as one.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:29.880
It established that right

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.040
in cases involving raising children.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:34.740
That parents have the right
to teach them another language

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.720
or send them to private school.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.090
And in cases involving having children,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:39.923
that the government

00:01:39.923 --> 00:01:42.480
could not sterilize convicted criminals.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.710
And in the landmark 1965 case,
Griswold versus Connecticut,

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.960
which was based on these earlier rulings

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.470
that overturned a Connecticut
ban on contraception.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.330
In the opinion, the Supreme Court ruled

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.600
that marital privacy, even
though not mentioned explicitly

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.010
in the Constitution

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.590
is one of those unenumerated
rights protected

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.360
by the Ninth Amendment.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.250
And that right meant using birth control

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.720
was a private decision

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.030
and couldn't be banned by the state.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.400
- There was a lot of
different kind of ideas

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.110
of privacy that flowed form Griswold.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.720
- [Narrator] Cases that expanded
the right to birth control,

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.490
the right to same-sex relations,

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.140
and eventually marriage

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.730
were based on these cases

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.970
that established privacy
as an unenumerated right.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.350
- [Mary] And that was
sort of a constellation

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:28.140
of privacy interests
that arose in that way.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.610
- [Narrator] The right
to an abortion was one

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.700
of those what was established
based on these rulings.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.410
- The phrase in Roe v. Wade,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.240
it says that the right to privacy

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.160
that was identified in Griswold

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.920
is broad enough to encompass the decision

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.160
to end an unwanted pregnancy.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.810
- [Narrator] The Roe
opinion mentioned that right

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:49.290
could also be found in another
amendment, the Fourteenth,

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.060
which says no one shall be deprived

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.220
of liberty without due process.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.210
- It says we believe that's
based on the liberty clause

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.860
of the Fourteenth Amendment

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.980
or the due process clause,
another name for that clause.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.430
Roe is from a tradition
that looks at these concepts

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:08.880
of liberty, and equality very broadly.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:09.936
Okay?

00:03:09.936 --> 00:03:12.630
It applies them very
broadly to new circumstances

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.660
and to a society that is different

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.630
than the society that we
had in the 18th century.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.600
But two of the justices
thought that was too broad

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.890
of an interpretation.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:25.110
- The dissenters in 1973 said

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:28.110
that the Constitution is
silent on this matter.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.180
It doesn't say anything
about abortion one way

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.013
or the other,

00:03:31.013 --> 00:03:33.540
and this court has no business interfering

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.610
with the legislature's judgment

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:38.250
about how to regulate a pregnancy.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.160
And that's pretty much the reasoning

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.680
that the court has adopted now.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:44.910
- [Narrator] In overturning Roe,

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.310
the court wrote that
"an unenumerated right

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.600
should be deeply rooted

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.240
in this Nation's history and tradition."

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.890
And ruled that the Constitution

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.410
does not confer a right to abortion.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.770
- The methodology is called originalism

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.780
where you stick very closely

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.110
to what you think the authors

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.840
of the passage understood it to mean,

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.500
what the public understood
the passage to mean

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.360
at the time it was adopted.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:13.650
- [Narrator] But some legal experts

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.180
and civil rights activists

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.280
are concerned about how this methodology

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.530
could be applied to some
of these other rulings

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:21.960
involving privacy, like in Griswold

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.090
or in Obergefell versus Hodges,

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.390
which legalized same-sex marriage in 2015.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.220
But the majority wrote that those cases

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.040
do not destroy potential
life like abortion.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.200
They even wrote, "Nothing in this opinion

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.000
should be understood to cast doubt

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.390
on precedents that do
not concern abortion."

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:40.680
But the dissenters argued

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.301
that even if the
majority's word is trusted,

00:04:43.301 --> 00:04:46.350
that law has a way of
evolving without regard

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.150
to original intentions.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.460
And whatever today's majority might say,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.920
one thing really does lead to another.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:57.090
- This broader approach to
applying the liberty concepts

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.280
and equality concepts of the Constitution,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.110
the current majority of the Supreme Court

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.030
is very skeptical of that.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.880
And that doesn't mean that
they are eager necessarily

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:07.380
to overrule those precedents.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.540
There are different
conditions and different facts

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.250
that apply to each of them.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.620
But as a matter of principle, yes,

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.350
those would be on shaky ground.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.590
The idea that they would
be overruled wholesale

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.680
the way that Roe versus
Wade has been overruled,

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.420
a little harder to imagine

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:28.080
because the social acceptance is broader.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.900
- The court is certainly saying
this is just about abortion

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.900
but there's a breadcrumb
trail the court is leaving

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.120
in the future if it wants
to go further than abortion,

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.420
and I don't think we can
rule that out indefinitely.

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:40.770
- [Narrator] Experts like Ziegler say

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:42.630
there's not immediate prospect

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.360
that these other rulings
would be reversed.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.880
But this Supreme Court
views unenumerated rights

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.980
much differently than previous courts,

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.802
and that may play out in future cases.

00:05:52.802 --> 00:05:55.469
(pensive music)

